{
  "id": "COMPOUND:30153 - PROTEIN:6323",
  "verbalization": "Methyl Lucidenate Q appears to interact with DNA polymerase iota through a multi-step pathway, where it targets a common enzyme, CMP-N-acetylneuraminate-beta-1,4-galactoside alpha-2,3-sialyltransferase. This enzyme is subsequently targeted by two different compounds: (3aR,6S,6aR)-3,6-bis(3,4,5-trimethoxyphenyl)-1,3,3a,4,6,6a-hexahydrofuro[3,4-c]furan and 1-Hydroxycyclocolorenone. Both of these compounds are implicated in the regulation of DNA polymerase iota, suggesting a potential mechanism involving the modulation of sialyltransferase activity, which could influence DNA synthesis or repair processes. The evidence points to a possible dependency on these intermediates, highlighting a complex network of interactions that may underlie the observed effects of Methyl Lucidenate Q on DNA polymerase iota. The integration of these pathways underscores the role of sialyltransferases in the regulation of DNA polymerase iota, offering a plausible mechanism for the drug's interaction with this enzyme.",
